      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A \"force majeure\" clause in a commercial lease agreement typically excuses parties from fulfilling their contractual obligations when an extraordinary event beyond their control prevents them from operating, such as natural disasters, war, or government actions. If a sudden and prolonged interruption incident is covered by this clause, the tenant may be relieved from paying rent or may be granted an extension to fulfill their lease obligations, depending on the specific wording of the clause. Always consult with a legal professional to understand the full implications for your specific scenario.",
