    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense from a strictly commercial standpoint. I'm just trying to get a better handle on the nuances for my client. If we can't rely on force majeure because these disruptions are technically \"foreseeable business risks,\" what's the typical legal argument used to characterize these high-frequency, erratic interruptions? Is there a way to frame them as something more substantial than just \"bad luck\" or \"operational friction\" to make a case for relief?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Legally, erratic operational interruptions due to supply chain issues are typically considered high-frequency business risks that tenants can generally manage as part of doing business. Since these inconveniences stem from both the market and other external factors, they often don't meet the criteria for force majeure since they aren't generally outside an involved party's control or entirely unforeseeable. To frame these as more substantial, you would typically argue they have reached a level of severity or persistence that fundamentally impedes the tenant's broader commercial purpose or results from a supply chain shock large enough to disrupt the local market's entire rhythm of operations. However, this argument is challenging because it requires demonstrating a systemic impact affecting the broader industry, rather than an isolated business's bad luck. Legal implications typically revolve around seeking rent abatement or negotiating modified terms that provide some form of relief during periods of impact. But these arguments are less about force majeure and more about the equity and reasonableness of the lease terms in light of the tenant's unforeseen difficulties.",
      "source": "target",
